**Read:** [Physical Geology, second edition — Steven Earle](../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX), geological time, scale, and rate examples across the opening chapters; [Laboratory Manual for Introductory Geology — Deline, Harris, and Tefend](../sources/Introductory-Geology-Laboratory-Manual.lingua#r_7G68RZ432EQWTB84DSVJ4JCZ2Z), the measurement/map-scale preparation.

A grain, outcrop, basin, plate, and planet reveal different features and processes. State the observation window and resolution before comparing them. A contact drawn at regional scale does not claim that its position is known equally well at the scale of a foundation excavation. Temporal averaging can hide short events or pauses in a long record.

Use consistent units and distinguish an age reference from a duration. A rate estimate needs a measured change and an identified interval with uncertainties and model assumptions. Erosion, uplift, subsidence, sedimentation, and plate motion refer to different quantities and reference frames; one cannot replace another merely because all use length/time units.

For invented constant-rate examples, 50 mm yr⁻¹ corresponds to 50 km per million years, while 3 mm yr⁻¹ corresponds to 3 km per million years. Extrapolation over a geological interval assumes constancy or a justified average. The arithmetic does not establish that an actual process persisted at that rate.

**Work:** convert a map scale, a grain-size measure, and a geological rate with independent magnitude checks. Compare two observations averaged over different spatial/time windows and explain why their reported rates may differ. Define an uncertainty and a reference frame for one proposed comparison.

Follow [Read graphs, interpolate, and question a visual claim](../../../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/foundations/Graphs-and-interpolation.lingua#r_2GDCZSQKNW5BCGN7B5D9G36HK2) for the shared treatment of coordinates, axes, interpolation, and visual claims. Its geology continuation returns to map reading; its quantitative continuation remains available. Introduce the mathematical preparation actually used without requiring every field of mathematics before approaching a geological question.

